## The verified ticket

A ticket is a local Markdown task in the `{{ shape.drafts_source }}` source, written to
`{{ drafts_root }}/{{ shape.tasks_directory }}/{{ run }}/{{ shape.tickets_directory }}/<root-cause>{{ shape.ticket_suffix }}` — qualified id
`{{ shape.ticket_id }}` — where `<root-cause>` is a kebab-case slug.

- It carries **no `project`**, so it lands on the board as a standalone item.
- **Its `repositories` lists every repository its fix changes, each once, as a normalized
  origin**, and those repositories decide the board it is filed on. A fix that changes one
  repository lists that one, and the record's `repository` — the repository its issue is
  filed in — names it too: on a GitHub board its issue is created in that one repository
  and added to the board as an item, and that repository must belong to the board's owner, as
  `github.com/<owner>/<name>`. A repository one of the board's routes names —
  `github.com/petsinc/*`, which goes to Hello Patient's Linear — is filed as an issue of the
  source that route names instead, whatever its owner.
- **A ticket listing several repositories is filed in the board's default repository rather
  than in any one of them**, as the store files a task naming several: on a GitHub board its
  issue is created in the repository that board's configuration names, and its record's
  `repository` names that default repository — `{{ validate }}` refuses the ticket naming it
  otherwise. It takes a route only when every repository it lists matches that route, so a
  ticket listing a `github.com/petsinc/*` repository beside one elsewhere stays on
  `{{ board }}`; one a route takes to a board filing no issue in a repository, such as Hello
  Patient's Linear, names one of the repositories it lists as its record's `repository`.
- Its title is `<repository name>: <the invariant in one line>`, at most
  {{ shape.title_limit }} characters, where the repository name is the last segment of `repository`,
  and its `<root-cause>` slug names the invariant rather than a file.
- **Its status is the board's to decide.** A new ticket is `{{ shape.proposed }}`, a proposal
  awaiting the user's decision, which the board shows as `Proposal` and Linear as `Proposed`; a
  person moving it to `Todo` is what accepts it. A ticket the board already holds carries the
  status the board holds it at, so a copy never undoes that decision: a ticket the board
  holds at `Deferred` (Linear's `Backlog`) is copied carrying `{{ shape.deferred }}`. A ticket this run withdraws is
  `{{ shape.withdrawn }}`, which the board holds as closed as not planned, unless the board shows it
  as accepted or deferred: this run never withdraws a deferred item or an accepted one, so
  copy nothing, leave the local ticket as it is, and report that you would have withdrawn
  it and why. No issue is ever deleted.
- **Before every copy, run `{{ board_status }} --board {{ board }} <path of the ticket>`** (adding
  `--withdraw` for a ticket this run withdraws, before you change that ticket), write the
  word it prints as the ticket's `status`, then validate the ticket. It reads the
  board item's `{{ shape.priority_field }}`, its record's `{{ shape.estimate_field }}` and its comments in the
  same step, and writes the ticket's `{{ shape.estimate_field }}`, its estimate line and its
  `{{ shape.priority_field }}` as the rule above decides, which the copy carries onto the board. It exits
  {{ shape.exits.sound }} with that word; {{ shape.exits.unplaced }} when the board holds the item at a status no ticket carries;
  {{ shape.exits.protected }} for a withdrawal of an item the board shows as accepted or deferred; {{ shape.exits.outside_owner }} when
  the ticket's repository is not one of the board's owner and no route of the board sends it
  elsewhere, before anything is asked of the board; {{ shape.exits.not_accepted }} when a `{{ shape.dependency_field }}` entry does not resolve on the board as the
  dependency rule below states, naming every such entry and what the board holds; and
  {{ shape.exits.misbound }} when the ticket's board item cannot be established as its binding, as the binding
  rule below states. On any of these refusals, copy nothing and report what it printed.
- **A refusal is reported, never worked around.** When `board-status` exits {{ shape.exits.outside_owner }}, or
  `{{ copy }}` refuses the ticket — for a repository the token cannot see, or one the board
  will not create an issue in — copy nothing for that ticket, never retry it with
  `repositories` removed or changed to get it filed, and report what was printed.
- Its front matter carries the `{{ shape.record_key }}` record with every key present, and its body
  the headings {{ shape.headings }}, in that order, each with content. `created_by_run` is this
  run, and `owning_runs` includes it.
- **`## Root cause` is its labelled parts**, {{ shape.root_cause_parts }}, in that order: the
  invariant or guarantee that is missing or broken, in words that do not depend on which file
  a run saw, then one bullet per location that contributes to it, each naming its repository
